---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { publishedLocations } from '../lib/publish';
import { deriveQuality, entryContentHash } from '../lib/quality';
import { AMAZON_TAG, site } from '../config';

const locations = await publishedLocations();
const total = locations.length;
const audioCount = locations.filter((l) => l.data.audio).length;
const verified = locations.filter(
  (l) => deriveQuality(l.data.factcheck, entryContentHash(l)).verifiedPublic
).length;
---

<Base
  title="About — PNW Historical Explorer"
  description="How this guide is researched, how sources are graded, what the verification badge means, and how the site pays for itself."
>
  <section class="hero" style="padding-block:clamp(2rem,1rem+3vw,3.25rem)">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow light">About</p>
      <h1>How this guide works</h1>
      <p>{site.tagline}</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>What this is</h2>
        <p>
          A field guide to {total} places across the Pacific Northwest that are worth pulling off
          the road for: mill towns that vanished, airfields that made a first, forts that never
          fired a shot in anger, and landscapes that carry more history than a marker can hold.
          {audioCount} of them have a narrated version you can save and listen to where there is no
          signal.
        </p>

        <h2>How the history is sourced</h2>
        <p>
          Every factual claim is meant to trace to a graded source. Sources are ranked in four
          tiers: primary and official records first (agency documents, National Register
          nominations, Library of Congress, the Coast Guard, state archives), then peer-reviewed
          scholarship, then editorially reviewed secondary sources such as HistoryLink and The
          Oregon Encyclopedia, and tribal institutions for their own history.
        </p>
        <p>
          Wikipedia is never treated as an authority here. It is a finding aid — useful for
          locating a document, never cited as the document.
        </p>
        <p>
          Where the record contradicts itself, the guide says so and names both sources. Where the
          record is thin, it says that too. Uncertainty stated plainly is more useful than
          confidence that turns out to be borrowed.
        </p>

        <h2>What the verification badge means</h2>
        <p>
          {verified} of {total} guides currently carry the “researched and sourced” badge. It means
          a specific thing: someone worked through the entry claim by claim against graded sources,
          recorded how many claims were cited, and found no open questions and no neutrality
          problems. It does <strong>not</strong> mean every sentence is guaranteed true.
        </p>
        <p>
          The badge also expires by itself. Each verified entry stores a fingerprint of the text it
          was checked against; editing the text changes the fingerprint and the badge drops off
          automatically until someone reviews it again. A verification claim that outlives the
          words it verified is worse than no claim at all.
        </p>

        <h2>Working offline</h2>
        <p>
          Pages, images, and map tiles are stored on your device as you browse, so a guide you
          looked at with signal is still there when you arrive without any. Audio narrations are
          larger, so they are saved only when you play one or press “Save for offline” — worth
          doing before you leave the last bar of coverage.
        </p>

        <h2>Privacy</h2>
        <p>
          There is no login, no account, and no advertising network. If you use “find places near
          me”, the coordinates stay on your device and are used to sort a list; they are never
          transmitted. Visitor numbers are counted with GoatCounter, which sets no cookies and
          collects no personal data — which is why there is no cookie banner to dismiss.
        </p>

        {AMAZON_TAG && (
          <>
            <h2>Affiliate disclosure</h2>
            <p>
              Book recommendations link to Amazon with an Associates tag, and a qualifying purchase
              earns a small commission at no extra cost to you. Books are chosen because they are
              the best sources on the subject; the link is added afterwards, and it never decides
              what gets recommended.
            </p>
          </>
        )}

        <h2>Corrections</h2>
        <p>
          If something here is wrong, it should be fixed. Local knowledge and family records catch
          things that published sources miss, and a correction with a source behind it is always
          welcome.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
